.Dd January 6, 2026
.Dt HISTOGRAM 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm HISTOGRAM_DECLARE ,
.Nm HISTOGRAM_LIN_GENERATE ,
.Nm HISTOGRAM_EXP_GENERATE
.Nd implementations of histogram data structures for tracking metrics
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/histogram.h
.Fn HISTOGRAM_DECLARE "name" "max_buckets"
.Fn HISTOGRAM_LIN_GENERATE "name"
.Fn HISTOGRAM_EXP_GENERATE "name"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These macros define histogram data structures for tracking metrics
with compile-time constant bucket counts.
.Pp
The
.Fn HISTOGRAM_DECLARE
macro defines a histogram structure containing a
.Vt uint64_t
array of
.Fa max_buckets
buckets.
.Pp
The
.Fn HISTOGRAM_LIN_GENERATE
macro generates functions for a linear histogram where values map directly
to bucket indices.
Values greater than or equal to
.Fa max_buckets
are clamped to the last bucket.
Linear histograms are ideal for tracking small integer values such as
segment counts.
.Pp
The
.Fn HISTOGRAM_EXP_GENERATE
macro generates functions for an exponential (log2-based) histogram where
values are mapped to buckets using the floor of the logarithm base 2.
Exponential histograms are ideal for tracking values over wide ranges
such as bandwidth (Mbps), latency (microseconds), or byte counts.
.Pp
Both generate macros create:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent
.It
An increment function
.Fn "name##_inc"
that increments the appropriate bucket for a given value.
.It
A weighted increment function
.Fn "name##_inc_weighted"
that adds a weight value to the appropriate bucket for a given value.
.El
.Pp
Histogram structures must be zero-initialized before use.
Use
.Fn bzero
or
.Fn memset
to initialize the structure:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bzero(&hist, sizeof(hist));
.Ed
.Pp
In the macro definitions,
.Fa name
is the name of the histogram type to be defined.
The generated structure will be named
.Fa name
and the generated functions will be prefixed with
.Fa name
followed by an underscore.
.Fa max_buckets
is a compile-time constant specifying the number of buckets in the histogram.
.Sh LINEAR HISTOGRAM EXAMPLE
.Bd -literal
#include <sys/histogram.h>

/* Define a linear histogram type with 64 buckets */
HISTOGRAM_DECLARE(segments_hist_type, 64);
HISTOGRAM_LIN_GENERATE(segments_hist_type);

/* Declare a histogram instance */
static struct segments_hist_type segments_hist;

/* Initialize the histogram (zeros all buckets) */
bzero(&segments_hist, sizeof(segments_hist));

/* Increment bucket for value 5 */
segments_hist_type_inc(&segments_hist, 5);

/* Increment bucket for value 10 with weight 1500 (bytes) */
segments_hist_type_inc_weighted(&segments_hist, 10, 1500);

/* Access bucket values directly */
uint64_t count = segments_hist.buckets[5];
.Ed
.Sh EXPONENTIAL HISTOGRAM EXAMPLE
.Bd -literal
#include <sys/histogram.h>

/* Define an exponential histogram type with 16 buckets */
HISTOGRAM_DECLARE(gput_hist_type, 16);
HISTOGRAM_EXP_GENERATE(gput_hist_type);

/* Declare a histogram instance */
static struct gput_hist_type gput_hist;

/* Initialize the histogram (zeros all buckets) */
bzero(&gput_hist, sizeof(gput_hist));

/* Increment bucket for value 50 */
gput_hist_type_inc(&gput_hist, 50);

/* Increment bucket for value 1000 */
gput_hist_type_inc(&gput_hist, 1000);

/* Access bucket values directly */
uint64_t count = gput_hist.buckets[10];
.Ed
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
The histogram macros generate type-specific structures and functions at compile time.
This approach provides type safety and allows the compiler to optimize
the generated code.
.Pp
Linear histograms use direct indexing:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
if (value >= max_buckets)
    value = max_buckets - 1;
buckets[value]++;
.Ed
.Pp
Exponential histograms use log2 mapping to determine the appropriate bucket.
.Sh SEE ALSO
(No cross-references.)
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm histogram
macros first appeared in Netflix OCA firmware in 2026.

